:{}:
# rpi4b nfsroot/netboot init script, run by `psh -i /etc/rc.nfsroot` from the
# boot script. `W` = wait for the command to exit, `X` = start in the
# background, `T` = open a controlling terminal.
#
# By the time this runs the boot script has already brought up everything: the
# devfs bind, the RAM /tmp, posixsrv, lwip, the NFS takeover of "/" and the
# device drivers. So this script deliberately repeats NONE of that -- re-binding
# /dev or restarting posixsrv here would collide with the live ones.
#
# Its only job is the wall clock. The Pi4 has no RTC, so time() starts at the
# epoch: every file the system creates is stamped 1970, and two libc/misc stat
# tests fail for that reason alone (they compare a fresh file's timestamps with
# time(NULL) while the NFS server stamps real epoch times). One SNTP round trip
# against the lab server fixes both. `-t 5` bounds the wait: an absent server
# costs five seconds and then boot continues to the shell.
#
# The server address matches the lab host the boot script already hardcodes for
# the NFS export lines. Change both together if the lab addressing changes.
# X, not W: the shell must never depend on the clock step finishing. A boot was
# observed where ntpclient did not complete and this script therefore never
# reached the psh launch below, leaving no way in. The clock is then set a moment
# after the prompt appears instead of just before it.
X /bin/ntpclient -s 10.42.0.1 -t 5
# Controlling terminal on the console, then the interactive shell (the same
# hand-over the sd variant's rc.psh does -- `psh -i` runs a script and exits,
# so the script has to spawn the interactive shell itself).
T /dev/console
X /bin/psh
